meta {
  name: WxC User Phone: API Dial
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: https://webexapis.com/v1/telephony/calls/dial
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
  Authorization: Bearer {{personaltoken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "endpointId": "{{callingDeviceId}}",
    "destination": "+18005532447"
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: false
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Dials a destination using a person's *personal* virtual line (their softphone, desk phone, and
  mobile client will all ring, unless a specific `endpointId` is given) — the Call Controls "Dial"
  endpoint.

  **Parameters/Headers**
  - `Authorization` — bound to `{{personaltoken}}` (placeholder). Use a personal access token from
    the user who owns the phone. This can be copied from developer.webex.com or generated
    programmatically as per the guide https://developer.webex.com/admin/docs/authentication. This
    request needs a token with the `spark:calls_write` scope, owned by the user whose line is being
    controlled.

  **Body** (`body:json`)
  - `endpointId` — bound to `{{callingDeviceId}}`; the specific device to ring. Omit this field
    entirely to ring all of that user's registered devices at once.
  - `destination` — the number or address to dial.

  **Usage**: The response returns a `callId` — save it to use with "WxC User: API Hang Up" or other
  call-management operations.

  See https://github.com/TeamCCEP/DeskphoneControl for more info and a little web app that will
  help visualise these APIs.

  Reference: https://developer.webex.com/docs/api/v1/call-controls/dial

  ---
  *Documentation written by AI (Claude) as a companion to the WebexOne 2026 API Overview session.*
}
